separate Nanite's geometry selection from

4
00:00:17,153 --> 00:00:22,380
Lumen's lighting updates. We begin with
three representations of the same wall.

5
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:28,241
Screen-space reflections read visible
color and depth. Off-screen information

6
00:00:28,241 --> 00:00:32,697
requires another representation.
Camera depth, world-space distance

7
00:00:32,697 --> 00:00:37,220
and voxel lighting may all appear
as grids but store different data.

8
00:00:37,480 --> 00:00:43,352
A depth image summarizes the front surface
at each pixel. Its contents change with the

9
00:00:43,352 --> 00:00:49,020
camera. Repeated reads cannot fully recover
hidden surfaces that were never stored.

10
00:00:49,380 --> 00:00:53,797
A distance field stores distance
from spatial points to a nearby

11
00:00:53,797 --> 00:00:58,214
surface, possibly with a sign
distinguishing inside and outside.

12
00:00:58,214 --> 00:01:02,700
Our wall diagram shows distance
layers rather than camera pixels.

13
00:01:02,990 --> 00:01:09,887
Voxels partition volume. They may store
occupancy, material or directional lighting.

14
00:01:09,887 --> 00:01:16,210
The term voxel alone does not specify
a reflection or illumination algorithm.

15
00:01:16,690 --> 00:01:21,631
Ambient occlusion summarizes visibility,
whereas global illumination includes

16
00:01:21,631 --> 00:01:27,150
transported light and color. Darkening a
corner does not fully compute color bleeding.

17
00:01:27,450 --> 00:01:31,371
Distance-field AO can query
world-space data. Screen-space

18
00:01:31,371 --> 00:01:35,089
GI uses color and depth but
retains screen limitations.

19
00:01:35,089 --> 00:01:39,550
Moving a hidden object off-screen
tests that information boundary.

20
00:01:39,650 --> 00:01:44,130
The gray view comes from enabling the
Mesh Distance Fields visualization.

21
00:01:44,130 --> 00:01:48,732
Unlike the original material-color
view, rocks and walls appear rounder and

22
00:01:48,732 --> 00:01:53,335
simpler. As the camera moves, examine
the representation of narrow gaps and

23
00:01:53,335 --> 00:01:58,060
broad walls together. In the earlier
diagram, the ray advanced using distance

24
00:01:58,060 --> 00:02:02,846
to the surface rather than reading
triangle colors. This view represents scene

25
00:02:02,846 --> 00:02:07,388
geometry. It is separate from Nanite
cluster colors and cached lighting in

26
00:02:07,388 --> 00:02:11,990
the Surface Cache, so these different
visualizations must not be conflated.

27
00:02:12,107 --> 00:02:17,435
Coarse distance fields or voxels
can lose thin walls or merge

28
00:02:17,435 --> 00:02:23,287
nearby surfaces. Coverage, detail
and update cost must be balanced.

29
00:02:23,450 --> 00:02:26,898
The view now switches to Global
DistanceField. Follow the same rocks

30
00:02:26,898 --> 00:02:30,499
with the camera and compare this
coarse representation with the earlier

31
00:02:30,499 --> 00:02:34,150
per-mesh fields. Similar colors do
not make the storage units identical.

32
00:02:34,333 --> 00:02:39,405
Rays can query scene triangles
beyond the screen, but that does not

33
00:02:39,405 --> 00:02:44,553
make the work cheap. We first need
a way to skip unrelated geometry.

34
00:02:45,303 --> 00:02:51,802
A ray is an origin plus a direction
multiplied by a parameter. Before

35
00:02:51,802 --> 00:02:58,583
testing every triangle, we can test
boxes enclosing groups of triangles.

36
00:02:58,873 --> 00:03:04,953
A BVH organizes bounding volumes
hierarchically. Missing a parent box allows

37
00:03:04,953 --> 00:03:12,233
its descendants to be skipped. We distinguish
visited and rejected branches in the diagram.

38
00:03:12,533 --> 00:03:19,532
An axis-aligned box can be tested using
ray intervals between the two bounds of

39
00:03:19,532 --> 00:03:26,353
each axis. Without a common interval
across all axes, the ray misses the box.

40
00:03:26,503 --> 00:03:30,684
Our example uses intervals of two
to six, three to five, and four

41
00:03:30,684 --> 00:03:34,736
to seven. Their overlap is four
to five. Replacing one interval

42
00:03:34,736 --> 00:03:38,723
with eight to nine removes the
overlap and rejects the branch.

43
00:03:38,907 --> 00:03:45,357
A box hit does not guarantee a
geometry hit. Bounds conservatively

44
00:03:45,357 --> 00:03:51,807
retain candidates; leaf geometry
still needs intersection testing.

45
00:03:51,917 --> 00:03:55,882
Closest-hit and visibility
queries have different goals.

46
00:03:55,882 --> 00:04:00,414
One needs the nearest valid
surface, while another can terminate

47
00:04:00,414 --> 00:04:05,017
after finding an appropriate
blocker. Their costs need not match.

48
00:04:05,270 --> 00:04:11,446
Poor bounds can group unrelated objects and
leave excessive candidate work. Building a

49
00:04:11,446 --> 00:04:17,550
better hierarchy also costs time. Traversal
quality and construction cost are linked.

50
00:04:17,850 --> 00:04:22,880
Hardware ray tracing in 2018 accelerated
portions of intersection work.

51
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:28,690
It did not make traversal, material
evaluation and scene updates universally free.

52
00:04:29,437 --> 00:04:37,077
A mesh can appear in multiple scene instances.
Geometry-level and scene-instance acceleration

53
00:04:37,077 --> 00:04:43,157
structures separate those responsibilities,
commonly called BLAS and TLAS.

54
00:04:43,703 --> 00:04:50,504
Moving an instance differs from deforming
its triangles. The required structure

55
00:04:50,504 --> 00:04:57,563
updates change, and update versus rebuild
conditions depend on the engine and API.

56
00:04:58,167 --> 00:05:04,605
A cheap update does not always preserve
optimal traversal. Deformation can reduce

57
00:05:04,605 --> 00:05:10,567
bound quality, trading lower update
cost for more expensive future queries.

58
00:05:11,067 --> 00:05:16,650
Hybrid rendering combines a rasterized base
with selected ray-traced effects such as

59
00:05:16,650 --> 00:05:22,167
shadows or reflections. Different tasks
can use different methods within one frame.

60
00:05:22,517 --> 00:05:26,466
In this engine example, a point light
beside a blue box is adjusted while

61
00:05:26,466 --> 00:05:30,144
we examine the shadow direction and
edge. First locate the light and

62
00:05:30,144 --> 00:05:33,985
object, then watch where the shadow
changes. The visible result and the

63
00:05:33,985 --> 00:05:38,042
acceleration structure we drew earlier
are different layers of information.

64
00:05:38,042 --> 00:05:41,937
This capture alone does not measure
the cost of updating that structure.

65
00:05:42,317 --> 00:05:46,673
Battlefield V's initial DXR
reflections illustrate that hybrid

66
00:05:46,673 --> 00:05:51,100
approach. They are distinct from
later full path-tracing modes,

67
00:05:51,100 --> 00:05:55,597
and game release dates should be
separated from feature updates.

68
00:05:56,127 --> 00:06:01,474
Access to off-screen lights does not
remove noise from sparse rough-reflection

69
00:06:01,474 --> 00:06:07,027
samples. The next question is how to
produce a stable estimate with limited work.

70
00:06:07,797 --> 00:06:12,017
When many directions or paths
contribute, samples can estimate

71
00:06:12,017 --> 00:06:16,305
their combined effect. The
estimator must account for selection

72
00:06:16,305 --> 00:06:20,117
probability, not simply average
arbitrary chosen values.

73
00:06:20,443 --> 00:06:25,737
Two equally weighted teaching values,
two and eight, average to five.

74
00:06:25,737 --> 00:06:30,800
Sampling eight more often without
compensation biases the estimate

75
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:35,403
upward. Better selection still
requires appropriate weights.

76
00:06:35,553 --> 00:06:42,568
Monte Carlo estimators divide contributions
by sampling density. Rare, large contributions

77
00:06:42,568 --> 00:06:49,193
can have large weights. Distribution design
therefore matters alongside sample count.

78
00:06:49,627 --> 00:06:54,325
Under independent sampling and finite
variance, standard error scales inversely

79
00:06:54,325 --> 00:06:58,844
with the square root of sample count.
Halving it requires roughly four times

80
00:06:58,844 --> 00:07:03,067
as many samples. Correlated or filtered
samples need separate analysis.

81
00:07:03,493 --> 00:07:08,103
Accumulation can add information
in a static scene. In motion,

82
00:07:08,103 --> 00:07:12,267
the same pixel coordinate can
belong to another surface.

83
00:07:12,267 --> 00:07:16,653
Image coordinates and surface
correspondence are different.

84
00:07:16,967 --> 00:07:22,018
Now examine the glass, metal and
hanging lights in the attic. Reflection,

85
00:07:22,018 --> 00:07:27,207
shadow and indirect-light controls are
separate. As the camera moves, first

86
00:07:27,207 --> 00:07:32,605
watch which surfaces of the same object
become visible. Having several effects

87
00:07:32,605 --> 00:07:37,587
in one image does not mean they use
the same samples or the same filter.

88
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:43,753
Two separated frame planes help visualize
correspondence. Object motion and

89
00:07:43,753 --> 00:07:49,660
camera motion determine which previous
location might provide usable history.

90
00:07:50,503 --> 00:07:55,045
DLSS 2, introduced in 2020, combines
a lower-resolution current image,

91
00:07:55,045 --> 00:07:59,651
motion information and temporal
feedback to produce a higher-resolution

92
00:07:59,651 --> 00:08:04,063
output. Its approach differs from
the earliest DLSS implementations.

93
00:08:04,330 --> 00:08:08,088
Reconstruction consumes engine-rendered
information. It is not the

94
00:08:08,088 --> 00:08:11,846
same stage as casting additional
rays. Savings from lower internal

95
00:08:11,846 --> 00:08:15,490
resolution must be considered
together with reconstruction cost.

96
00:08:15,500 --> 00:08:19,591
In the Deliver Us The Moon comparison,
watch the helmet rim, glove and

97
00:08:19,591 --> 00:08:23,975
lines on the rear wall together. Check
the displayed quality mode and scene

98
00:08:23,975 --> 00:08:28,066
before comparing the results. Looking
only at large clear surfaces can

99
00:08:28,066 --> 00:08:32,216
miss small boundaries. We also retain
the condition that the source has

100
00:08:32,216 --> 00:08:36,600
already enlarged these views, rather
