117
00:09:48,177 --> 00:09:51,734
We keep the discussion at renderer
inputs, outputs and failure

118
00:09:51,734 --> 00:09:55,349
conditions rather than neural
architecture. Missing information

119
00:09:55,349 --> 00:09:58,677
is not guaranteed to be recovered
correctly in every case.

120
00:09:59,183 --> 00:10:03,991
The menus in this engine demonstration
show DLSS quality modes, DLAA and

121
00:10:03,991 --> 00:10:08,866
NIS separately. Even when examining
the same reflective sphere and edges,

122
00:10:08,866 --> 00:10:13,540
first read which option changed.
Reconstruction from lower resolution,

123
00:10:13,540 --> 00:10:18,481
stabilizing edges at native resolution
and spatial upscaling are distinct.

124
00:10:18,481 --> 00:10:23,823
Similar names do not establish identical
input resolution or use of past frames.

125
00:10:24,417 --> 00:10:30,707
DDGI updates spatial probes with rays and
lets surfaces query the resulting irradiance

126
00:10:30,707 --> 00:10:36,997
field. This can avoid independently tracing
long indirect-light paths for every pixel.

127
00:10:37,183 --> 00:10:40,612
Sunlight and shadows move in the
two boxes. The next edit changes

128
00:10:40,612 --> 00:10:43,987
the indirect-light method from
None to Plugin, and color returns

129
00:10:43,987 --> 00:10:47,521
to the previously dark walls.
Observe the action and result without

130
00:10:47,521 --> 00:10:50,843
treating this demonstration as
a measurement of update latency.

131
00:10:51,047 --> 00:10:56,967
Probes are world-space locations rather
than camera pixels. Their directional ray

132
00:10:56,967 --> 00:11:02,887
results can update with lighting and serve
multiple surfaces as the camera moves.

133
00:11:03,133 --> 00:11:07,113
This example moves and resizes the
box for the indirect-light volume.

134
00:11:07,113 --> 00:11:11,380
First look at the room and walls within
it, then inspect where the spatial

135
00:11:11,380 --> 00:11:15,359
points appear when probes are
visualized. This placement differs from

136
00:11:15,359 --> 00:11:19,339
a grid attached to camera pixels.
Enlarging the volume alone does not

137
00:11:19,339 --> 00:11:23,433
automatically solve visibility behind
walls or detail on small objects.

138
00:11:23,707 --> 00:11:29,771
Unconditionally blending nearby
probes can leak light across walls.

139
00:11:29,771 --> 00:11:36,107
Distance and visibility information
are needed to constrain the query.

140
00:11:36,453 --> 00:11:41,874
Our diagram contrasts distance-only blending
with occlusion-aware weighting across a wall.

141
00:11:41,874 --> 00:11:46,933
The example explains the query, without
claiming a universal implementation formula.

142
00:11:47,397 --> 00:11:52,798
Wider probe spacing smooths small
features. Denser probes cost storage

143
00:11:52,798 --> 00:11:58,277
and updates, while partial updates
add convergence-time considerations.

144
00:11:58,433 --> 00:12:02,917
With probes shown as spheres, we can
inspect their spacing and the wall colors

145
00:12:02,917 --> 00:12:07,170
together. Separate the choice to place
more points from the choice of when

146
00:12:07,170 --> 00:12:11,596
to recompute each value. The spheres
are debug representations for inspecting

147
00:12:11,596 --> 00:12:15,965
stored illumination, not new physical
lights inserted into the room. Connect

148
00:12:15,965 --> 00:12:20,333
them to the interpolation diagram while
considering room size and occlusion.

149
00:12:20,517 --> 00:12:25,850
Dynamic updates do not imply instantaneous
perfect results. Moving lights

150
00:12:25,850 --> 00:12:31,257
and wall boundaries help separate
latency, leakage and computational cost.

151
00:12:31,633 --> 00:12:36,183
In this city example, inspect the probes
between buildings. The grid count shown in

152
00:12:36,183 --> 00:12:40,733
the source is specific to this demonstration,
rather than a default for every game.

153
00:12:41,137 --> 00:12:48,943
ReSTIR's 2020 paper addressed dynamic
direct lighting by reusing candidates over

154
00:12:48,943 --> 00:12:56,457
space and time. Later GI and path-tracing
variants are distinct developments.

155
00:12:56,650 --> 00:13:01,517
In NVIDIA’s 2021 demonstration, examine
the windows, signs and lighting around cars

156
00:13:01,517 --> 00:13:06,326
on the boulevard. As the camera and cars
move, watch which lights appear on nearby

157
00:13:06,326 --> 00:13:11,252
surfaces. We can see the many-light result,
but the process of replacing a candidate

158
00:13:11,252 --> 00:13:16,178
inside a small reservoir is not displayed.
After inspecting the result, we calculate

159
00:13:16,178 --> 00:13:20,870
the selection probabilities for weights
one and three in an independent diagram.

160
00:13:21,080 --> 00:13:25,824
A reservoir keeps a selected
candidate and summary information

161
00:13:25,824 --> 00:13:31,028
rather than all candidates. Its
weighted selection is probabilistic,

162
00:13:31,028 --> 00:13:35,160
not an instruction to always
keep the brightest light.

163
00:13:35,430 --> 00:13:39,542
In our example, weights one and
three give a cumulative weight

164
00:13:39,542 --> 00:13:43,852
of four. The second candidate
replaces the first with probability

165
00:13:43,852 --> 00:13:48,030
three quarters, leaving the first
with probability one quarter.

166
00:13:48,133 --> 00:13:52,137
This illustrates weighted selection
only. A final lighting estimator

167
00:13:52,137 --> 00:13:55,965
also accounts for proposal
distributions and represented samples.

168
00:13:55,965 --> 00:13:59,733
The replacement probability alone
is not the complete estimator.

169
00:13:59,987 --> 00:14:05,869
Neighboring and historical candidates broaden
the search. Their usefulness can change with

170
00:14:05,869 --> 00:14:11,687
surface position, orientation and visibility,
so reuse requires appropriate reevaluation.

171
00:14:11,947 --> 00:14:17,195
Closing a door can occlude a previously
useful light. Rechecking current visibility

172
00:14:17,195 --> 00:14:22,127
explains why reusing a candidate differs
from copying an old brightness value.

173
00:14:22,260 --> 00:14:28,403
ReSTIR concerns sample selection and
reuse; denoising concerns stabilizing

174
00:14:28,403 --> 00:14:34,380
evaluated results. They can work
together without being interchangeable.

175
00:14:35,120 --> 00:14:41,521
Efficient lighting does not eliminate geometry
and visibility work. Frustum culling rejects

176
00:14:41,521 --> 00:14:47,360
objects outside the view, while occlusion
culling targets hidden objects within it.

177
00:14:47,693 --> 00:14:53,948
Being inside the view volume does
not guarantee visibility. A wall can

178
00:14:53,948 --> 00:15:00,203
hide an in-frustum object. The two
tests address different conditions.

179
00:15:00,217 --> 00:15:04,421
Now move close to the statues and inspect
the moments when foreground geometry

180
00:15:04,421 --> 00:15:08,464
hides the building behind it. Distinguish
objects within the camera frustum

181
00:15:08,464 --> 00:15:12,777
from surfaces actually visible in the
final image. Even when the source counters

182
00:15:12,777 --> 00:15:16,928
change, this capture does not isolate
the cost of a particular culling stage.

183
00:15:16,928 --> 00:15:20,917
It connects the candidate-removal
diagram to actual changes in visibility.

184
00:15:20,950 --> 00:15:25,765
Early depth testing can avoid shading
occluded samples when pipeline

185
00:15:25,765 --> 00:15:30,367
conditions permit. Depth writes
or shader side effects can change

186
00:15:30,367 --> 00:15:35,040
those conditions; not every hidden
fragment is automatically free.

187
00:15:35,450 --> 00:15:39,760
A hierarchical depth buffer
summarizes regions for conservative

188
00:15:39,760 --> 00:15:44,002
occlusion tests. Its reduction
and comparison rules must match

189
00:15:44,002 --> 00:15:47,970
the depth convention, including
reversed depth where used.

190
00:15:48,293 --> 00:15:55,064
Previous-frame depth can become stale when
doors open or the camera moves quickly.

191
00:15:55,064 --> 00:16:01,173
Conservative handling prevents newly
visible objects from being discarded.

192
00:16:01,483 --> 00:16:07,243
These culling methods resemble BVH rejection
in spirit but act at different pipeline

193
00:16:07,243 --> 00:16:12,523
stages. Identify which query they
accelerate and which data they pass onward.

194
00:16:12,900 --> 00:16:16,468
Inside the cave, nearby rocks hide
parts of the exit and building.

195
00:16:16,468 --> 00:16:20,361
Moving the camera reveals these
surfaces in turn. Connect this motion to

196
00:16:20,361 --> 00:16:24,253
the earlier distinction between
candidates within the image and surfaces

197
00:16:24,253 --> 00:16:28,470
currently visible. Passing through the
exit also changes the building’s screen

198
00:16:28,470 --> 00:16:32,417
size and occlusion. These observations
illustrate the visibility problem,

199
00:16:32,417 --> 00:16:36,201
while not isolating the execution
time of an individual culling stage.

200
00:16:36,201 --> 00:16:40,580
The internal calculation can be followed
separately in the depth-pyramid diagram.

201
00:16:41,203 --> 00:16:47,893
Mesh shaders provide a pipeline for generating
and processing grouped geometry. That

202
00:16:47,893 --> 00:16:54,583
role is separate from indirect illumination
and can support geometry-work reduction.

203
00:16:55,173 --> 00:17:01,782
Variable Rate Shading changes how
frequently shading is evaluated in selected

204
00:17:01,782 --> 00:17:08,133
regions. Shading frequency and output
pixel count are separate dimensions.

205
00:17:08,460 --> 00:17:15,210
A two-by-two teaching block can share one
shading evaluation. Platform support and

206
00:17:15,210 --> 00:17:21,960
pipeline behavior still matter, and this
is not a promise of fourfold performance.

207
00:17:22,230 --> 00:17:29,117
Virtual texturing manages residency of needed
pages rather than keeping every texture region

208
00:17:29,117 --> 00:17:35,330
resident. It concerns data availability,
not learned generation of texture content.

209
00:17:35,333 --> 00:17:41,093
Sampler feedback can report texture
demand. Knowing the demand does not make

210
00:17:41,093 --> 00:17:46,853
data immediately available; streaming
latency and residency remain relevant.

211
00:17:47,367 --> 00:17:52,890
Geometry groups, shading rate and
texture pages target different resources.

212
00:17:52,890 --> 00:17:58,487
Nanite, VRS and DLSS should not be
collapsed into one resolution technology.

213
00:17:58,950 --> 00:18:03,849
On this ornamented wall, distinguish the
shapes of small ridges from the surface

214
00:18:03,849 --> 00:18:08,747
colors. Moving closer and sideways changes
the occlusion between narrow gaps and

215
00:18:08,747 --> 00:18:13,523
